A1. Bubble water. ~ CHAMP.GNE
A9. A talkative kind of operation. ~ MAJOR
A14. Here's where you get rubber next to nothing. ~ PARA
A15. Fast worker. ~ DOER
A16. Underworld fiction. ~ ALIBI
A17. Winglike, as of an angel. ~ ALAR
A18. When the idle rich and the laborers meet. ~ DAWN
A19. Home of God's Frozen People. ~ IGLOO
A20. A terribly dry state. ~ SOBRIETY
A22. Makes both ends meet. ~ UNITES
A23. Oriental country poeticized. ~ IND
A24. There's quite a lot to this sort of person. ~ OBESE
A26. Food raisers. ~ WAVES
A29. How to raise the lid. ~ PRY
A30. A quiet hanging. ~ ARRAS
A34. Old as they make 'em. ~ AGED
A35. This is beastly but Dumb Dora says only a millionaire can afford one. ~ YAK
A36. What the lovely optician's daughter made of herself. ~ VISION
A37. Lizzie's skin. ~ TIN
A38. There's quite a fall to this. ~ DEW
A39. Here's a twister for you. ~ EEL
A40. Goal for an amateur. ~ PRO
A41. What the wise police force does to criminals in Chicago. ~ ELUDES
A43. Drowning place for tramp's sorrows. ~ VAT
A44. To no. ~ VETO
A45. Squeak remover. ~ RESIN
A46. Parked the bones. ~ SAT
A47. The vegetable the gold digger loves. ~ CARAT
A48. Baby bringer. ~ STOIC
A50. Diary of any old wet smack. ~ LOG
A51. The first example of international armament scrapping. (It'd knock your eye out back in the '90's.) ~ HATPIN
A54. The game the letter-carrier plays on his day off. ~ ANAGRAMS
A59. These cover a lot of ground. ~ ACRES
A60. Early American hair-cutters. ~ UTES
A61. Here's a bitter one for you. ~ ALOE
A62. The Austin-size whale. ~ SMELT
A63. Warms the eggs. ~ SETS
A64. This is in the air. ~ NEON
A65. Women supporters. ~ HEELS
A66. Separators of donkies from sheep and goats. ~ ASSORTERS

D1. These may prove cool summers (abbr.). ~ CPAS
D2. This went to the saint's head. ~ HALO
D3. A sandy fellow. ~ ARAB
D4. Ready for Reno. ~ MARRIED
D5. Totalled. ~ .DDED
D6. The tin type. ~ GOAT
D7. Jimmy Walker occasionally drops in here. ~ NEWYORK
D8. Constant ocean traveler. ~ ERN
D9. A big blowout on a yacht. ~ MAINSAIL
D10. This place has a dark past and present and future. ~ ALGIERS
D11. To hand the gate but not the ring. ~ JILT
D12. This keeps blowing about Leopold Stokowski. ~ OBOE
D13. Spanish flowers. ~ RIOS
D21. Them as gits. ~ INS
D22. Unctuous Edward, initially. ~ UE
D25. At. ~ BY
D26. Swamp juice. ~ WATER
D27. The kind of girl you can't get your hands on. ~ AGILE
D28. This woman will never be penalized for holding. ~ VENUS
D29. Troublemaker in a taxicab. ~ PAW
D31. This kind of tomatoes makes better throwing than ripe ones. ~ RIPER
D32. The main stem that touches the heart. ~ AORTA
D33. To tilt the nose. ~ SNOOT
D35. Once upon a time there was a girl who meant "Yes" and said this (fairy tale). ~ YES
D36. Dog plumber. ~ VET
D38. Nerve wrackers. ~ DENTISTS
D39. To gobble the groceries. ~ EAT
D42. To dissipate. ~ DISPELL
D43. Beats it. ~ VACATES
D44. The hobo in Boston. ~ VAGRANT
D46. This is O. K. in Spain. ~ SI
D47. A worker in the machinery of capitalism. ~ COG
D49. Where to stay with a horse. ~ ON
D50. There's a catch to this. ~ LASSO
D51. What the Gleaners ate for dinner. ~ HASH
D52. This is the nuts. ~ ACME
D53. Dogs go around kicking at this. ~ TREE
D55. Fishy propositions. ~ NETS
D56. The safe side. ~ ALEE
D57. To park the sidewheeler. ~ MOOR
D58. A little change for a Jap (plur.). ~ SENS
D60. The suburbs of New York. ~ USA


